{
 "help": "usage: wgsextract align [-h] [--r1 R1] [--r2 R2]\n                        [--platform {illumina,dnbseq,ultima,pacbio,hifi,clr,ont,nanopore}]\n                        [--aligner {auto,bwa,bwa-mem2,bwa-mem3,minimap2,pbmm2}] [--fast]\n                        [--prefer-native-gpu | --no-prefer-native-gpu]\n                        [--dense-suffix-array | --no-dense-suffix-array] [--sample SAMPLE]\n                        [--format {BAM,CRAM}] [--output-profile {default,archive,calling}]\n                        [--input-ref INPUT_REF] [--help-all]\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --r1 R1               Read 1 FASTQ file\n  --r2 R2               Read 2 FASTQ file (optional)\n  --platform {illumina,dnbseq,ultima,pacbio,hifi,clr,ont,nanopore}\n                        Sequencing platform; selects platform-specific alignment tooling (default:\n                        detected from read groups or read names, else illumina).\n  --aligner {auto,bwa,bwa-mem2,bwa-mem3,minimap2,pbmm2}\n                        Aligner to use. Auto uses bwa-mem2 for short reads when the reference has\n                        its index (see `ref index`), else bwa; for long reads pbmm2 for PacBio\n                        when installed, otherwise minimap2. bwa-mem3 (wgse-bwa-mem3 on Apple\n                        Silicon and Linux x86-64, else bwa-mem3 from PATH or its Pixi environment)\n                        writes bwa's exact records faster, from bwa-mem2's index.\n  --fast                Align short reads with bwa-mem3's --fast speed preset (implies --aligner\n                        bwa-mem3), about twice as fast. Its alignments differ from BWA's\n                        (docs/hardware.md has a measurement); the run warns (bwa_mem3_fast).\n                        Without it bwa-mem3 writes BWA's exact records.\n  --prefer-native-gpu, --no-prefer-native-gpu\n                        Prefer native GPU alignment. Windows CUDA is automatic when it fits; --no-\n                        prefer-native-gpu forces CPU there.\n  --dense-suffix-array, --no-dense-suffix-array\n                        Have wgse-bwa (0.11.0 or later) read the reference's dense suffix-array\n                        file, built by `ref dense-sa`: the same alignments, faster, holding the\n                        file in RAM (0.8 GB for hs38). Used only when wgse-bwa aligns: otherwise\n                        (another aligner, such as wgse-bwa-mem3 standing in, no file, or no such\n                        wgse-bwa) the run warns (dense_suffix_array_unused) and aligns without it.\n                        Default: config dense_suffix_array, else off.\n  --sample SAMPLE       Sample name for the read group (default: the reads' file name).\n  --format {BAM,CRAM}   Output format (default: BAM)\n  --output-profile {default,archive,calling}\n                        Lossless output: default keeps --format; archive selects CRAM 3.1 (GATK\n                        >=4.7); calling selects CRAM 3.0. Default: config output_profile, else\n                        default. See docs/ont.md.\n  --input-ref INPUT_REF\n                        Reference FASTA a CRAM --r1 was encoded with (default: --ref when its\n                        sequence names, lengths and MD5s match the CRAM).\n  --help-all            Show this help with the advanced options too, and exit.\n\nglobal options:\n  -i/--input, -o/--outdir, --genome, --ref, -t/--threads, -m/--memory, --json, --quiet, --debug,\n  --config and the other global options are accepted before or after the command; 'wgsextract\n  --help' lists them all.\n\n5 advanced options not shown; 'wgsextract align --help-all' lists them.\n\nexamples:\n  wgsextract align --r1 sample_R1.fastq.gz --r2 sample_R2.fastq.gz --ref hs38.fa\n  wgsextract align --r1 hifi_reads.fastq.gz --preset map-hifi --ref hs38.fa\n\noutputs (in the output directory unless noted):\n  <reads stem>_aligned.bam or .cram (+ index).\n\nneeds:\n  samtools; depending on options and inputs, also: bcftools, bwa, bwa-mem2,\n  bwa-mem3, curl, gatk, minimap2, pbmm2, sambamba, samblaster; hours on a 30x\n  genome.\n",
